.Dd August 31, 2026
.Dt XO-LOGGER 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xo-logger
.Nd make entries in the system log, with libxo-style format strings
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 46Ais
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl H Ar hostname
.Op Fl h Ar host
.Op Fl P Ar port
.Op Fl p Ar priority
.Op Fl S Ar addr:port
.Op Fl t Ar tag
.Op Ar message Op Ar argument...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is a command-line-compatible replacement for the traditional
.Xr logger 1
utility, built on the
.Nm libxo
library.
It reads a message, either from the command line, a file, or its
standard input, and passes it to the system log module,
.Xr syslog 3 ,
via
.Nm libxo Ns 's
.Xr xo_syslog 3
function, which generates an RFC 5424-style structured log message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger 'disk failure on da0'

  syslog:
    <13>1 2026-08-31T10:22:03.512-0400 myhost phil 4207 - - disk failure on da0
.Ed
.Pp
Every option below that also appears in the traditional
.Xr logger 1
utility behaves the same way, so existing scripts that invoke
.Xr logger 1
can generally be switched over to
.Nm
unchanged.
The
.Ar message
argument's
.Nm libxo
formatting is the one deliberate extension beyond that compatibility,
described next.
.Ss Message Format and Arguments
Unlike stock
.Xr logger 1 ,
the
.Ar message
argument may use the
.Nm libxo
field-descriptor format string syntax described in
.Xr xo_emit 3 ,
consuming any trailing command line arguments as field values.
This lets a single log message carry both a human-readable rendering
and a set of structured, named fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger 'Disk {:disk} is at {:percent/%d}%\\n' /dev/da0 97

  syslog:
    <13>1 2026-08-31T10:22:03.512-0400 myhost phil 4207 - \\
        [- disk="/dev/da0" percent="97"] Disk /dev/da0 is at 97%
.Ed
.Pp
If no format directives are needed, an ordinary string works fine, as
in the first example above;
.Nm
does not require the caller to opt into the extended syntax.
.Pp
If a
.Ar message
argument is given on the command line, it takes precedence over
.Fl f Ns / Ns Fl -filename ;
if neither is given,
.Nm
reads lines from its standard input, one log message per line.
This matches the way
.Xr logger 1
chooses among its three input sources, except that
.Xr logger 1
favors
.Fl f
over a message argument, while
.Nm
favors the message argument, since a format string with field values
on the command line is otherwise indistinguishable from plain text.
.Ss Input Sources
.Fl f Ar file , Fl -filename Ar file
reads the message from a file, instead of the command line, sending
one log message per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --filename /var/log/build-errors.txt --tag builder
.Ed
.Pp
Each line of the file becomes an independent syslog message; none of
the
.Nm libxo
field-descriptor handling is applied to file input, since there are
no further command line arguments to supply field values.
.Pp
When neither a
.Ar message
argument nor
.Fl -filename
is given,
.Nm
reads from its standard input instead, again treating each line as a
separate message:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % tail -F /var/log/build.log | xo-logger --tag builder
.Ed
.Ss Priority: Facility and Severity
.Fl p Ar priority , Fl -priority Ar priority
sets the facility and/or severity of the message, given together as
a single
.Dq facility.severity
pair:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --priority local0.error \\
      'Disk {:disk} is at {:percent/%d}%\\n' /dev/da0 97
.Ed
.Pp
Either half of the pair may be omitted, in which case it defaults
independently;
.Dq local0
alone sets just the facility, while
.Dq .error
alone sets just the severity.
With neither half given,
.Nm
defaults to
.Dq user.notice ,
the same default
.Xr logger 1
uses.
A purely numeric priority is also accepted, as is generated by the
.Fn LOG_MAKEPRI
macro.
.Pp
The complete set of recognized facility and severity names can be
listed with
.Fl -list-facilities
and
.Fl -list-severities :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --list-facilities
  auth
  authpriv
  cron
  ...
  % xo-logger --list-severities
  emerg
  alert
  crit
  ...
.Ed
.Ss Message Identity
.Fl t Ar tag , Fl -tag Ar tag
marks every message with the given tag, which appears in the syslog
output as the message's APP-NAME:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --tag backup-cron 'nightly backup finished'
.Ed
.Pp
If
.Fl -tag
is not given,
.Nm
defaults to the invoking user's login name, falling back to
.Dq -
if that cannot be determined.
.Pp
.Fl H Ar hostname , Fl -hostname Ar hostname
overrides the HOSTNAME field of the message, which otherwise
defaults to the local host's name.
This is useful when
.Nm
is being used to relay messages on behalf of some other system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --hostname sensor-42.example.com \\
      'Temperature {:temp/%d} out of range' 105
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl i
is accepted, for compatibility with
.Xr logger 1 Ns 's
.Fl i
flag, but has no effect; the messages
.Nm
generates always include a process ID in the PROCID field, so there
is nothing extra for
.Fl i
to turn on.
.Ss Echoing to Standard Error
.Fl s , Fl -print ,
in addition to the system log, prints the message to standard error,
prefixed with the message's tag, the same way
.Xr logger 1 Ns 's
.Fl s
does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --print --tag build 'compile failed'
  build: compile failed
.Ed
.Pp
This is handy for interactive use, or when a script wants both a
system log entry and immediate console feedback.
.Ss Remote Delivery
By default,
.Nm
writes to the local system log socket, just like
.Xr logger 1 .
.Pp
Giving
.Fl h Ns / Ns Fl -host
instead sends the message via UDP to that host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --host loghost --tag backup 'Backup complete'
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl -host
accepts either a hostname or an address, resolving a hostname to
whichever address family responds;
.Fl 4 Ns / Ns Fl -inet4
and
.Fl 6 Ns / Ns Fl -inet6
restrict that resolution to IPv4 or IPv6 addresses only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --inet6 --host loghost.example.com 'started'
.Ed
.Pp
A hostname may resolve to more than one address.
By default only the first is used;
.Fl A Ns / Ns Fl -all-addresses
sends the message to every address the host resolves to:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --all-addresses --host loghost-cluster.example.com \\
      'cluster event'
.Ed
.Pp
.Fl P Ar port , Fl -port Ar port
selects the destination port on
.Fl -host ,
given as either a service name or a number, defaulting to 514, the
standard syslog port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --host loghost --port 1514 'started on alternate port'
.Ed
.Pp
An unrecognized service name falls back to 514, with a warning.
.Pp
.Fl S Ar addr:port , Fl -source Ar addr:port
binds the outgoing socket to a specific local address and port
before sending, which is useful on multi-homed hosts where the log
server expects traffic from a particular interface:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --host loghost --source 192.0.2.10:0 'started'
.Ed
.Pp
IPv6 source addresses must be bracketed, e.g.
.Dq Li [::1]:1514 .
.Pp
A
.Fl -host
value that begins with
.Pa /
is treated instead as a filesystem path to an
.Dv AF_LOCAL
socket, such as
.Pa /var/run/log ,
letting
.Nm
target a non-default local socket the same way it targets a remote
one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  % xo-logger --host /var/run/log.otherjail 'jail-local message'
.Ed
.Pp
This remote delivery support is implemented as a family of
.Fn xo_log_set_*
functions in the
.Nm libxo
library itself, documented alongside
.Xr xo_syslog 3 ,
so other logging clients can be built on the same functionality
without re-implementing DNS resolution or socket management.
.Ss Command Line Options
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl 4 , Fl -inet4
When resolving the host given by
.Fl h Ns / Ns Fl -host ,
consider only IPv4 addresses.
.It Fl 6 , Fl -inet6
When resolving the host given by
.Fl h Ns / Ns Fl -host ,
consider only IPv6 addresses.
.It Fl A , Fl -all-addresses
Send the message to every address that
.Fl h Ns / Ns Fl -host
resolves to, rather than just the first.
.It Fl f Ar file , Fl -filename Ar file
Read the message from
.Ar file
instead of the command line, sending one log message per line.
Ignored if a
.Ar message
argument is also given.
.It Fl H Ar hostname , Fl -hostname Ar hostname
Use
.Ar hostname
for the message's HOSTNAME field, instead of the local host's name.
.It Fl h Ar host , Fl -host Ar host
Send the message to
.Ar host
instead of the local system log socket, via UDP.
.Ar host
may also be a filesystem path to an
.Dv AF_LOCAL
socket, such as
.Pa /var/run/log .
.It Fl i
Accepted for compatibility with
.Xr logger 1 ;
has no effect, since generated messages always include a process ID
in the PROCID field.
.It Fl P Ar port , Fl -port Ar port
Send the message to
.Ar port
on
.Ar host ,
given as a service name or a number.
An unrecognized service name falls back to the standard syslog port
(514), with a warning.
Defaults to 514.
.It Fl p Ar priority , Fl -priority Ar priority
Set the priority of the message, either numerically or as a
.Dq facility.level
pair, e.g.
.Dq local0.error .
Either half may be omitted, e.g.
.Dq local0
or
.Dq .error .
Defaults to
.Dq user.notice .
.It Fl s , Fl -print
In addition to the system log, print the message to the standard
error output.
.It Fl S Ar addr:port , Fl -source Ar addr:port
Bind the outgoing socket to the given local address and port before
sending to
.Fl h Ns / Ns Fl -host .
IPv6 addresses must be bracketed, e.g.
.Dq Li [::1]:1514 .
.It Fl t Ar tag , Fl -tag Ar tag
Mark every message with the given tag.
Defaults to the invoking user's login name.
.It Fl -list-facilities
List all valid logging facility names and exit.
.It Fl -list-severities
List all valid logging severity (level) names and exit.
.It Fl -help
Display help text.
.It Fl -version
Display version information.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
  xo-logger 'Disk {:disk} is at {:percent/%d}%\\n' /dev/da0 97

  xo-logger -p local0.error -t diskmon \\
      'Disk {:disk} is at {:percent/%d}%\\n' /dev/da0 97

  xo-logger -h loghost -P 514 -t backup 'Backup complete'

  logger_input.sh | xo-logger -t backup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xo 1 ,
.Xr logger 1 ,
.Xr libxo 3 ,
.Xr xo_emit 3 ,
.Xr xo_syslog 3 ,
.Xr syslog 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared as a distinct command in libxo, having
previously been a mode
.Po
.Fl L Ns / Ns Fl -logger
.Pc
of the
.Xr xo 1
utility.
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm libxo
library was written by
